WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.075 --> 00:00:05.450
ALEXIS
MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE: Hello,

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.870
and good afternoon, Google IO.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.150
Thank you for being here.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:11.810
Thank you for everybody on
the live steam, as well.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:15.790
The cloud is what powers
an endless number of web

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:19.000
and mobile apps, and
that makes them magical.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.020
The easy access to potentially
huge amounts of compute,

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.520
storage, and to
machine learning APIs

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.637
is what makes the
cloud so special.

00:00:28.637 --> 00:00:30.220
And something that
you probably should

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.662
try to leverage in your
applications whenever possible.

00:00:33.662 --> 00:00:35.620
My name is Alexis, and
I'm a developer advocate

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.160
with Google Cloud.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.420
Now, a few questions for you.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:42.360
How many Android developers
do we have in the room?

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.840
How many web developers?

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.010
Anybody here has
used Firebase before?

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.020
A good number, that's great.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.090
Now, to be fair, server side--

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.980
or otherwise,
back-end development--

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.550
can look tedious to some folks,
or just not your cup of tea.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.020
And that's fine.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.560
Luckily, Google has
this rich, open,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.910
and developer-friendly cloud,
Google Cloud Platform, or GCP

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.260
for short.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.520
Let's take a look and
start with Firebase.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.520
You might know Firebase for
all the amazing features

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.880
it offers to enhance
your client code--

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.120
authentication, crash
reporting, analytics,

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.680
A/B testing, and the fairly
recently announced ML Kit.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.770
I'd like here to
cover everything that

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.760
is not a client-side feature.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.700
And that's because Firebase
is a wonderful way for Android

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.210
and web developers to
leverage Google Cloud Services

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:45.130
without exposing them
to all the knobs of GCP.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.980
So first, there's cloud storage.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:51.610
This will let you manipulate
user-generated files

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.120
and application files,
such as pictures, videos.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.370
Think of it as a
way to save files

00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:02.050
to the cloud for later retrieval
from any authorized device

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.360
or service.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:09.039
Next, Cloud Firestore
is the JSON database

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.490
for all your application data
with real time notifications.

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:18.130
This means that if a specific
value changes in Firestore,

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.220
all your connected
users can be notified.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.640
This is truly amazing.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.960
Cloud Functions is the easy
way to add server-side logic

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.680
without having to manage
any server or any cluster.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.260
Simply provide a piece of code.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.460
specify the event that triggers
the execution of that code.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:42.940
And that could be, for example,
an image uploaded to a bucket,

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.360
or some data changing
in Filestore.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.860
Finally, ML Kit for
Firebase offers the ability

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.130
to add machine learning
features to your apps using

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:59.270
APIs that run either in
the cloud or on device.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:03.670
So this includes text
recognition, face detection,

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.700
barcode scanning,
image labeling.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:10.160
And as announced at the
technical keynote yesterday,

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.290
we now have also something
called AutoML Vision Edge, as

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.240
well as on device translation.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.320
So why am I talking
about Firebase, here?

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.700
Well, it's simple.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.890
These features that
I've just described

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.540
are all based on Google
Cloud Platform, or GCP.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:32.130
And if you only remember one
thing from this presentation,

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.320
it should be that you
should start with Firebase--

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.270
if you haven't done so already--

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.730
and then grow to use GCP.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:42.160
When you create a
Firebase project,

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.610
it is, in fact, a GCP
project in every aspect.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:50.770
Resource grouping, identity
management, and billing.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:53.470
This gives you a great
way to easily use

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.060
some cloud services--

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.430
before you graduate
into using GCP--

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:59.300
when you need to.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.470
So let's talk about
Google Cloud Platform

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.870
and give you a sense
of how it can extend

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:08.800
what we've just briefly seen.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.150
So Google Cloud
Platform, GCP, is big.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.960
I mean, really big.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.140
We recently had a large
event in San Francisco called

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:23.010
Cloud Next with tens of
thousands of participants,

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:27.490
with some sessions just focusing
on one specific feature.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:31.260
So I can't possibly be
covering everything here.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:32.760
Instead, I'll try
to cover what I

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.100
believe might be useful
to you and give you

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.160
a few tips along the way.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.650
Maybe you already have some
sort of back end software,

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.120
and you're just
looking for a place

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.840
to host it, potentially,
in the cloud.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.410
So that will help you spend
less time on infrastructure

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:56.200
management, get potentially
better security,

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:00.050
and essentially spend more
time writing your app.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:02.650
If you are familiar
with virtual machines,

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:07.520
Compute Engine is a great
environment for you.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.500
Compute Engine, or GCE, offers
virtual machines, disks,

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.060
and networking.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.080
As you can see, we can go
from very small to very large

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.230
amounts of CPU and memory.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:25.290
And that can be
done using sliders,

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:29.250
as shown here, to get the
perfect configuration.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:31.680
You can also customize
your virtual machines

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.960
by adding GPUs and Cloud
CPUs, tensor processing units.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.510
There are many delightful
features about GCE,

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:41.760
but one of my favorites
is the little time

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.640
that it takes to
provision and boot a VM.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.430
But it's probably best if
I show you this in action.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:53.360
So with that, let's switch
to the demo machine,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.600
and let's create a VM.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.130
Let's take most of the defaults.

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:03.530
I will call this instance
one, just to make it more fun.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:05.210
Create this in Europe.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:08.450
And you can have
different presets.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.640
You can see the sliders
that I just talked about.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.690
You can change the image,
the OS that you're using.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:16.560
There are many to choose from.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.650
You can set identity and API
access settings and setup

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:23.763
a firewall to
allow or disallow--

00:06:23.763 --> 00:06:24.680
which is the default--

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.560
any HTTP or HTTPS traffic.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.190
So let's actually go ahead
and create that instance.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:34.850
So we're actually provisioning
here the instance.

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:38.750
We're creating this, in
this case, with a DBN image.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:43.190
With all the defaults, this
one has one CPU and, I think,

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.270
four gigabytes of memory.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.690
And it is being created,
as I said, in Europe.

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:48.410
And that's it.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.810
We have the VM that's
available, that

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.940
has an external IP address.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:55.855
And we can, at this point,
SSH into the machine.

00:06:55.855 --> 00:06:57.980
And notice, I didn't have
to install anything here.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:02.280
I'm just using a web browser,
clicking through a button,

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.180
and here I am, actually
connected to that machine that

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.060
didn't exist a few seconds ago.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:13.180
So I can look at it, and I
can do things such as apt-get.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:14.650
Update.

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:17.090
And I have a fully
working machine,

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:20.800
which I can resize,
which I can delete,

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:24.395
which I can recreate
very easily.

00:07:24.395 --> 00:07:26.020
So, with that, let's
go back to slides.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:32.480
This is just a few seconds
to get a new machine running

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:34.230
in a Google data center.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.620
You can also do this with
the command line, which

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.710
means you can easily automate
the process of spinning off

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:43.550
a machine, or multiple machines,
when you need them, and also

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:46.130
shut them down when you
no longer need them.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:53.100
There are many features
with Compute Engine, some

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.900
that I would like to point
out are live migration,

00:07:55.900 --> 00:08:00.000
which is really a unique feature
to migrate running applications

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.240
from one machine to another.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:08.090
Preemptible VMs offers
you short-lived, low cost

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:10.880
virtual machines, something
that I would recommend

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.940
that you use if your jobs,
your running processes,

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.870
can be interrupted.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:18.730
We also have some
automation features,

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:22.600
such as instance groups, to
administer virtual machines

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:26.920
in batches, to achieve
some level of scalability,

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:30.100
and even to do auto repair
on production instances.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.429
Now, let's switch gears
a little bit and talk

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:40.409
about storing data, and
databases in particular.

00:08:40.409 --> 00:08:44.770
Storing data is likely to be
vital for your application.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.460
You could, of course, spin
up a VM-- as we just did--

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.710
install your favorite
database there.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:54.540
But here, we'll go through some
cloud, or GCP native, storage

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:58.020
and database options,
just a few to give you

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:01.030
a sense of what's available.

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:05.470
Chances are you have files
that you would like to store.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.440
And cloud storage, in this
case, is a pretty obvious choice

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.000
here.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.600
You can put all of your files
into one or multiple buckets,

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:17.050
and prices for storing these
varies depending on what

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.740
we call the storage class.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:21.840
To give you a sense
of the cost, you

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.360
can host all of the
internet archive--

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.370
and that's about 10 petabytes--

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:31.980
for less than $100 per month,
all with no disk to manage.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:35.330
No capacity issue
to worry about.

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:38.490
So cloud storage offers
different classes, as I said,

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:40.140
of storage.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.380
These classes range
from having data

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:46.000
as close as possible
to the end user--

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.070
this is multi-regional--
to archival needs.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.190
The regional option
here is good for when

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:58.320
data needs to be close to the
machine that processes it.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.950
The long-term storage,
Nearline and Coldline,

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:04.680
are technologies that
come with retrieval cost

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.710
but are ideal for archival data.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:11.340
But really, best of all here
is that there's a single API

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:14.580
to manage the lifecycle
of your objects,

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:20.360
regardless of their
storage class.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:23.010
And you can move objects
from one class to the other,

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:24.980
so data that's no
longer used can

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:28.260
be pushed back to
something like, let's say,

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:31.860
the Coldline storage.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:36.420
Now, for user data, such as
their profile information,

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:39.490
the transactions associated
with those users,

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:42.010
you may want to use a
relational database.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:44.700
And rather than
setting up, managing,

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:46.950
and securing your
own installation,

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:49.960
you can use Cloud SQL instead.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:53.340
This will free you up
from monitoring uptime

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:56.880
of that database,
from managing backups,

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:00.580
and from applying
security patches.

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:02.830
Cloud SQL also
offers the ability

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:07.460
to define replicas for a
highly available setup.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:11.910
Their currently supported
databases are MySQL, Postgres,

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.650
and SQL Server coming soon.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:20.400
Now, NoSQL databases
are known to scale,

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:25.270
regardless of the amount of
data that you throw at them.

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:28.720
And Cloud Firestore
is no exception.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:34.700
In fact, this database offers a
JSON structure with no schema.

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:36.740
It is strongly consistent.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:38.630
It is indexable.

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:41.450
And it is serverless, meaning
that there is no infrastructure

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:44.690
to size, provision, or manage.

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:49.310
And it comes with great SDKs
for mobile and web development.

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:52.015
If you've ever used the real
time database for Firebase,

00:11:52.015 --> 00:11:53.390
it's all the good
things you know

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:59.000
with added strong consistency,
better querying capabilities,

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:02.000
multiple data centers
around the world, and still

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.267
the great notification
and offline capabilities

00:12:04.267 --> 00:12:04.850
that you know.

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:12.280
So which one do you choose?

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:15.360
Well, for files such as
images, PDF documents,

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:16.290
it's pretty easy.

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:20.960
Cloud storage Is most
likely what you need.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:23.780
For user data,
transactional information,

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:27.845
good old relational databases
offered in a Cloud SQL package

00:12:27.845 --> 00:12:31.260
are probably a good choice.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:33.710
If you need horizontal
scalability,

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:39.720
a schema-less database, change
notifications, mobile offline

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:43.680
support, you're probably looking
at Cloud Firestore, which

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:45.360
you can use, by
the way, regardless

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:47.310
of any other Firebase features.

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:55.170
Now, let's go beyond VMs and
into Cloud native solutions.

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.020
Now, don't get me wrong.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:58.670
Virtual machines are great.

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:02.570
But management remains your
responsibility-- provisioning,

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:06.110
patching the OS, updating
it, and all the securities

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:09.350
left for you to do, which
means time not spent

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:12.220
developing your app.

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:14.710
And also, the scalability
is pretty much vertical.

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:18.730
You can make the VM bigger,
but you can't really

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:23.050
play on the horizontal axis
with clustering technologies.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:25.120
And availability is
also something that

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:28.660
is hard to add after the fact.

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:32.190
So here, we'll talk about
cloud native approaches

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:33.890
to running your code--

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:39.570
Kubernetes, Cloud Functions, App
Engine, and the newly released

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:40.635
Cloud Run product.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:46.340
So maybe by a show of
hands here, how many people

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:50.600
know and use Docker containers?

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:51.245
Great.

00:13:51.245 --> 00:13:55.790
So containers are solving the
works on my machine problem

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:59.420
by packaging an app with
all of its dependencies--

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:01.160
including its runtime--

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:04.072
into a container.

00:14:04.072 --> 00:14:05.530
But if you use
containers, you also

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:10.840
probably know that that
does not solve all problems.

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:14.500
You still need to schedule
and scale those containers.

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:19.430
You need to manage their
health, monitor them, and more.

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:22.700
This is where schedulers,
such as Kubernetes,

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:25.210
come into the picture.

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:27.160
As the inventor of
Kubernetes, Google

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:31.810
offers Google Kubernetes
engine, or GKE, a fully managed

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:32.635
Kubernetes service.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:37.710
If you were starting
from virtual machines,

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:41.940
or from your own servers,
setting up Kubernetes clusters

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:44.340
would mean that you have to
deal with, well, actually

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:48.180
creating the virtual machines,
attaching some storage,

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:51.180
installing the actual
Kubernetes software,

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:54.640
setting up some
networking and security.

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:56.660
And that's a lot of work.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:59.890
Instead, with Google
Kubernetes Engine, GKE,

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:04.210
it takes one command line
and just a few minutes.

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:06.970
Once created, the
cluster is ready to host

00:15:06.970 --> 00:15:09.890
your containerized applications.

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:14.870
Kubernetes version upgrades,
auto repair of failing nodes,

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:17.630
and other features are provided
out of the box with GKE.

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:27.180
Now, while GKE offers a
wonderful, portable platform,

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:30.180
it comes with a requirement
to first containerize

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:32.420
your application.

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:35.990
It also still requires
creating and managing a cluster

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:37.020
with worker nodes.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:39.080
So this isn't really
serverless, which

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:43.900
we define as something that
has no server management, which

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.240
is fully secure by default--

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:48.480
this is not your problem--

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:52.050
and that has to pay per
use through auto scaling,

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.540
including scale to zero.

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:55.600
This all sounds nice.

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:59.970
So let's take a quick
look at some GCP products

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.610
that actually qualify
for this definition.

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:09.570
So an obvious and popular
example of serverless

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:13.320
is Functions as a
Service, or FAS.

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:15.480
And Cloud Functions is
a great implementation

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:17.250
of that paradigm.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:20.790
Simply upload some
code written in Python,

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:26.310
and go in Node, or even in Java,
along with its dependencies,

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:28.770
and define the
event that triggers

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:31.270
the execution of that code.

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:33.310
The events can be
anything from an HTTP

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:37.750
request coming in to a file
being uploaded to a bucket.

00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:39.730
Data changing in Firestore.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:44.640
Messages being posted
to a Pub/Sub topic.

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:46.950
Cloud Functions has
been used by customers

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:50.400
to implement everything
from, what we call, glue code

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:55.080
to a fully fledged microservices
based applications.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:55.950
But enough talking.

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:58.830
Let's see Cloud
Functions in action.

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:00.810
If we could go back
to the demo machine--

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:09.010
This is a function
written in Node

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:11.880
that will be triggered
when a file--

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:15.589
a picture, in this case-- is
uploaded to a specific bucket.

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:18.869
So we have an event
for a given bucket,

00:17:18.869 --> 00:17:20.700
for a given file
that has a name.

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:24.839
So the first thing we do here is
we download that file locally,

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:30.430
and then we use a library
called ImageMagick to actually

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:33.670
do the resize of that
picture, and to resize it

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:38.380
to width of 256 pixels
while preserving the ratio.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.970
We write those to a local file.

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:44.140
And if everything goes
well, we upload the result

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:49.330
with a prefix called
resize to that same bucket.

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:50.350
So this is the code.

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:53.610
There's some actual metadata
in terms of dependencies, where

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:58.020
we declare dependencies on Cloud
Storage, which we listen to,

00:17:58.020 --> 00:18:01.770
and the ImageMagick
version that we use.

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:05.100
We also define what triggers
the execution of this.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:09.300
And in this case, it's
an upload to this bucket,

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:11.340
which I can click on.

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:15.180
This bucket is empty, and
I suggest that we actually

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:18.930
upload a picture there.

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:22.660
So as we do this, we can look at
the picture that was uploaded.

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:26.640
And we can go back here.

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:29.977
And hopefully-- as I
refresh the bucket--

00:18:29.977 --> 00:18:31.560
we have a second
picture that's there.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:37.080
And that has been the resized
version of the initial one.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:39.390
So here you go.

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:41.310
These are Cloud
Functions in action.

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:43.630
And maybe we can move
now back to slides

00:18:43.630 --> 00:18:46.530
so I can tell you that
these are available in

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:48.000
multiple languages, as I said.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:52.020
This was Node, but you can
use Python, Go, and Java.

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:55.590
And there are many events that
can trigger them, not just file

00:18:55.590 --> 00:18:56.737
uploads.

00:18:56.737 --> 00:18:58.320
I think Cloud Functions
is the easiest

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:02.650
way to access one of the
many powerful GCP services,

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:07.710
from machine learning APIs, to
other storage and processing

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:08.370
solutions.

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:14.830
Now, as a developer,
you may want

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:17.800
to have yet even more
freedom in the languages

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:20.120
and the frameworks that you use.

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:21.910
And most importantly,
you may want

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:24.880
to hand over a
carefully crafted Docker

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:28.160
image instead of source code.

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:30.520
Cloud Run was
announced last month

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:32.870
at the Cloud Next conference.

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:36.530
And it is here to offer you
a truly serverless experience

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:40.210
for your stateless
HTTP container images.

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:42.910
So the events are
HTTP, and there

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:45.910
needs to be no state preserved
by the container for this

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:47.300
to work.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:49.900
But if that is something
that works for you, well,

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:53.800
simply build your image,
upload to a registry,

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:58.090
and create a cloud run
service using that container.

00:19:58.090 --> 00:20:00.610
At that point, your
app is now deployed

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:01.660
and running in the Cloud.

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:04.540
And you can forget about the
provisioning and managing

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:06.210
of servers.

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:08.010
Cloud Run does that for you.

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:12.150
It will automatically, and
quickly, scale up and down

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:14.370
based on the incoming traffic.

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:19.600
It will even scale to zero,
meaning, no traffic, no cost.

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:23.530
But even more, you can
use the same container

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:26.080
on your own GKE cluster.

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:31.840
If you really want to understand
and master the underlying

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.870
infrastructure, you can
do so since this is all

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:38.200
actually written on top of
an open source technology

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:41.710
called Knative, which
provides an abstraction layer

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:47.560
on top of Kubernetes to
provide a server environment.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:49.920
So choice is good, right?

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:51.240
Cloud Functions.

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:52.542
Cloud Run.

00:20:52.542 --> 00:20:53.250
Well, guess what?

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:56.050
There's even more choice.

00:20:56.050 --> 00:21:00.390
App Engine is the mother
of serverless at Google.

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:03.720
And it offers the ability
to build and host entire web

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:08.280
applications with multiple
services while still retaining

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:12.600
the source deployment approach,
all of that, obviously,

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:15.140
with serverless benefits.

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:18.520
It comes with
versioning built in.

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:22.050
It provides out-of-the-box
traffic splitting to implement

00:21:22.050 --> 00:21:25.740
things such as cannery
deployments or A/B testing,

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:28.470
all at the click of a button.

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:31.240
It also supports a
long list of languages,

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:36.540
including recent versions
of Python, Java, Go, PHB.

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:40.075
And we recently
announced Ruby, as well.

00:21:40.075 --> 00:21:41.700
So at this point,
you might be confused

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:44.080
about which one to use.

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:47.080
Let me suggest that you
think of it this way.

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:49.960
Which artifact would
you like to deploy?

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:52.420
Would you like to
give me a function?

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:55.735
Would you like to give me an
app that has multiple services?

00:21:55.735 --> 00:21:59.140
Or would you like to
give me a container?

00:21:59.140 --> 00:22:00.880
All of these are serverless.

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:03.010
All of these will scale to zero.

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:06.460
And we will manage the entire
infrastructure for you.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.930
OK, so intelligence.

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:16.050
Now, I'm not trying to imply
here that your apps are not

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:19.260
built by smart
developers, but instead,

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:22.260
that there are some
amazingly low hanging fruit

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:26.190
to make those
applications even smarter.

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:29.370
I'm talking here mostly
about machine learning,

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:33.810
and specifically about
easy-to-use APIs,

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:38.764
which any developer can call,
regardless of their ML skill

00:22:38.764 --> 00:22:41.390
set.

00:22:41.390 --> 00:22:44.060
We call these AI
building blocks.

00:22:44.060 --> 00:22:47.740
And they can group into
the following categories--

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:50.970
the Vision API and the
Video Intelligence API,

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:54.280
this is the site category.

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:57.390
We have the language category,
with natural language

00:22:57.390 --> 00:22:59.600
and translation APIs.

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:02.010
And we have the
conversation category,

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:04.950
with speech-to-text,
text-to-speech,

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:08.860
and Dialogflow APIs.

00:23:08.860 --> 00:23:12.040
I mentioned ML Kit
for Firebase earlier.

00:23:12.040 --> 00:23:14.770
What you have here is the
server-side machine learning

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:18.380
APIs that ML Kit actually uses.

00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:21.670
So these APIs are available
via RESTful endpoints,

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:25.510
making them easy to be
called from any part

00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:29.260
of your application
or your architecture.

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:33.850
Certainly, if you're building
mobile Android or iOS apps,

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:39.080
ML Kit for Firebase is a
great way to use these.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:42.850
So let's look at the Vision
API, which is one of those APIs.

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:47.930
And if we switch back
to the demo machine,

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:51.660
I can actually test this
API right from the browser.

00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:54.590
I can upload a
picture, same picture.

00:23:57.130 --> 00:23:57.890
Did I click on it?

00:24:01.570 --> 00:24:02.880
All right, let me refresh this.

00:24:06.790 --> 00:24:11.570
And I am not a robot, I hope.

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:14.510
So I'm sending this
to the Vision API,

00:24:14.510 --> 00:24:16.940
asking it to return
and tell me everything

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:19.790
it finds about that
picture, from entities

00:24:19.790 --> 00:24:27.060
to landmarks, to text, to
web properties and entities.

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:30.000
So it has detected that
this is indeed a landmark.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.610
This is Notre Dame, in Paris.

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:35.570
There are a bunch of
labels that it found.

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:37.640
This is machine
learning working for you

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:40.430
with a pre-trained model.

00:24:40.430 --> 00:24:42.800
There are web entities
with all the things

00:24:42.800 --> 00:24:45.330
that it finds on that picture.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:46.220
It even finds text.

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:48.650
If you were to zoom in, you
could see that the barge here

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:51.000
is called Nouvelle Seine.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:54.030
There's some image properties,
such as dominant colors.

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:57.300
And last but not least, there's
what we call Safe Search.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:25:02.100
Is this picture safe from a
adult, spoof, medical violence,

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:03.970
or racy point of view?

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:06.810
If you have a website that
has user generated content,

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:08.850
you probably owe it
to yourself to use

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:10.860
something, such
as the vision API,

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:12.450
to make sure everything
that's upload

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:16.650
is actually something you
can then show to other users.

00:25:16.650 --> 00:25:20.450
All of this is actually
the result of a request,

00:25:20.450 --> 00:25:23.570
asking for landmark
detection, face detection.

00:25:23.570 --> 00:25:25.430
We don't have any in this one.

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:28.700
Object localization,
we don't have any.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:31.730
But image properties,
crop hints, web detection.

00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:36.080
And the result is a JSON
document, which is actually,

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:38.660
parsed and presented
here, in this UI,

00:25:38.660 --> 00:25:42.200
but which, you typically would
be using in your application

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:45.470
to enhance your application.

00:25:45.470 --> 00:25:49.870
So if we move back to
slides, AI building blocks,

00:25:49.870 --> 00:25:53.380
in the form of API calls,
can be extremely powerful.

00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:55.060
And they're really
easy to set up.

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:59.210
Again, they're really
just an API call.

00:25:59.210 --> 00:26:03.580
So those APIs, such as
the vision API, are great.

00:26:03.580 --> 00:26:06.790
And we call these
pre-trained models,

00:26:06.790 --> 00:26:10.360
meaning that Google did the
heavy lifting of training

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:14.200
a model, leaving you with
the easy prediction part.

00:26:14.200 --> 00:26:15.160
Send an image.

00:26:15.160 --> 00:26:16.780
Get a result back.

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:20.140
But what if you wanted
to build your own model

00:26:20.140 --> 00:26:23.720
from your own data to better
fit your business needs?

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:27.280
This is where Cloud
AutoML comes in.

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:31.680
This is another part of
our AI building blocks.

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:34.870
AutoML lets you create
your own custom machine

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:37.570
learning models with an
easy-to-use graphical

00:26:37.570 --> 00:26:38.800
interface.

00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:41.800
These models can be specific
to your business needs

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:44.980
and trained with your own
data with minimal effort

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:46.315
and little to no coding.

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:54.420
Now, if you're an ML
developer, a data scientist,

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:57.200
you may want to have complete
control over the training

00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:59.660
and prediction phases.

00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:02.630
But you probably do not want
to have too much infrastructure

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:04.160
overhead.

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:07.700
And this is where the
cloud AI platform comes in.

00:27:07.700 --> 00:27:11.540
This is Google's data science
development environment.

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:14.610
We offer AI platform notebooks.

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:16.910
These are managed
Jupiter Labs notebooks

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:21.670
integrated with all the big
data products you find in GCP.

00:27:21.670 --> 00:27:26.140
Cloud TPUs, and the newly
announced Cloud TPUs pods,

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:28.180
are hardware
accelerators designed

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:31.510
to speed up machine learning
workloads for training,

00:27:31.510 --> 00:27:36.690
and prediction, and inference
programmed with TensorFlow.

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:40.610
Deep learning VM images are
pre-configured GCE virtual

00:27:40.610 --> 00:27:43.040
machines for deep
learning applications

00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:47.420
that use TensorFlow,
PyTorch, Sidekick Learn.

00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:51.740
And it's trivial to
add Cloud TPUs or GPUs

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:54.730
to these virtual machines.

00:27:54.730 --> 00:27:57.620
So Cloud AI platform
offers tools and products

00:27:57.620 --> 00:28:00.920
for probably the entire
lifecycle of machine

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:06.690
learning development if you'd
like to control everything.

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:09.750
Now, switching gears
a little bit here.

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:11.580
We've talked about
storing the data.

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:16.480
Let's talk now about
processing your data at scale.

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:21.400
Chances are your apps, web apps,
mobile apps, back end apps,

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:24.330
generate some valuable data.

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:28.240
And you would like to
turn this into insights.

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:30.370
This takes potentially
massive amounts

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:33.130
of compute power,
data processing

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:34.590
resources in general.

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:37.570
And the good news is that GCP is
really great and amazing place

00:28:37.570 --> 00:28:38.280
to do just that.

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:45.910
BigQuery is an amazing product
to make sense of your data.

00:28:45.910 --> 00:28:47.650
Simply send your data--

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:49.330
as much as you like--

00:28:49.330 --> 00:28:51.730
along with an SQL query.

00:28:51.730 --> 00:28:54.850
The data will be processed
in just a few seconds,

00:28:54.850 --> 00:28:58.400
thanks to BigQuery's
unique and really massive

00:28:58.400 --> 00:29:01.390
back end architecture.

00:29:01.390 --> 00:29:05.500
Cloud Dataflow is Google's
implementation of the Apache

00:29:05.500 --> 00:29:07.990
Beam programming model.

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:10.840
And it offers to
process and transform

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:17.140
massive amounts of data both in
batch and in streaming modes.

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:22.180
Cloud Dataproc is a hosted
Apache Hadoop and Spark version

00:29:22.180 --> 00:29:25.300
that will spin up a
fully managed cluster

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:27.760
in less than 90 seconds.

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:30.940
It will resize it dynamically
and offer, overall,

00:29:30.940 --> 00:29:34.990
great cost performance for
any Hadoop or Spark job

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:37.100
that you have.

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:40.890
Let's take a look at
a quick BigQuery demo

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:42.640
and move back to the
demo machine, please.

00:29:45.958 --> 00:29:54.910
So 400,000 GitHub
repositories, one billion

00:29:54.910 --> 00:29:56.950
files, and one question.

00:29:56.950 --> 00:29:59.230
Spaces or tabs?

00:29:59.230 --> 00:30:02.720
Well, we might have
the answer today.

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:05.780
We look at all of those files.

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:09.740
We just ignore those there
are less than 10 lines long.

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:13.810
And for every file, we
give a plus one if its tabs

00:30:13.810 --> 00:30:15.370
or a plus one if its spaces.

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:18.850
And if it's a mix, we just
decide to vote only one

00:30:18.850 --> 00:30:24.210
for whichever comes more
often, spaces or tabs.

00:30:24.210 --> 00:30:27.320
This is the GitHub,
the real data.

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:31.490
And what we have in this query
is the ability to run a query

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:34.430
against that table
and to actually--

00:30:34.430 --> 00:30:37.250
for every single line
in every single file--

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:40.100
run a regular expression,
counting the number of tabs

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:44.850
and the number of spaces,
and summing up all of this.

00:30:44.850 --> 00:30:47.900
So what I suggest is
we actually run this.

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:51.770
This will process 133 gigabytes.

00:30:51.770 --> 00:30:58.090
It shouldn't take more than,
say, 10, 12, 13 seconds,

00:30:58.090 --> 00:31:03.060
maybe, and hopefully
give you an answer.

00:31:03.060 --> 00:31:05.610
Down to 16 seconds.

00:31:05.610 --> 00:31:06.150
18.

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:07.890
That's not actually bad.

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:10.380
And so for every language,
every popular language--

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:14.250
and we base the query on the
extension of the files we found

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:16.840
in the repos--

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:18.240
we calculate a ratio.

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:22.230
Does it have more spaces or
more tabs for every language?

00:31:22.230 --> 00:31:27.180
And you could see that Java
tends to be more about spaces

00:31:27.180 --> 00:31:31.790
where Go is all
about tabs, clearly.

00:31:31.790 --> 00:31:33.180
So there you have it.

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:34.760
We actually know
the answer to one

00:31:34.760 --> 00:31:37.130
of the most crucial
and important questions

00:31:37.130 --> 00:31:40.040
we've had in this industry.

00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:42.390
Spaces.

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:44.690
Now, the amazing thing
here about the query

00:31:44.690 --> 00:31:47.660
is that we've analyzed
each file, again,

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:49.460
with a regular expression.

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:54.770
For 133 gigabytes of
code in 10-ish seconds

00:31:54.770 --> 00:31:56.780
in interactive
time, as we call it.

00:31:56.780 --> 00:31:59.000
There is no need to
go and grab a coffee.

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:01.230
Or come back the next
morning to get the answer.

00:32:01.230 --> 00:32:03.125
So you can iterate
them on your queries.

00:32:05.640 --> 00:32:08.240
And this is what the
graph looks like.

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:11.420
And credits should
all go to Felipe.

00:32:11.420 --> 00:32:12.917
And you can query this.

00:32:12.917 --> 00:32:14.000
All the details are there.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:15.350
You can run the query yourself.

00:32:15.350 --> 00:32:17.120
And you can look at
all the data and see

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:19.190
how it evolves through time.

00:32:19.190 --> 00:32:22.690
But the answer is spaces.

00:32:22.690 --> 00:32:24.370
So, to recap, we've
talked about how

00:32:24.370 --> 00:32:28.430
Firebase is a great foray
into Google Cloud Platform.

00:32:28.430 --> 00:32:30.730
We discussed virtual
machines and databases

00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:35.980
to bring the software stacks
that you love and know to GCP.

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:42.610
Next, we talked about
Cloud Native and serverless

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:45.710
and how to choose
the right solution.

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:49.540
And finally, we covered adding
machine learning and data

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:53.790
processing to your
apps and architectures.

00:32:53.790 --> 00:32:56.580
Before I close, let me
share a few tips and tricks

00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:57.900
as you get started with GCP.

00:33:00.970 --> 00:33:03.550
Google Cloud Console is
where you will likely

00:33:03.550 --> 00:33:05.620
spend a fair amount of
time exploring and using

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:06.710
the platform.

00:33:06.710 --> 00:33:08.980
This is where you
configure billing accounts.

00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:11.950
You create and you
manage projects.

00:33:11.950 --> 00:33:14.320
You manage all
your GCP resources,

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:18.160
regardless of the
data center location.

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:21.280
Every product and every
service has its own section

00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:22.280
in the console.

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:26.450
It has dashboards, detailed
configuration, and settings.

00:33:26.450 --> 00:33:30.850
There is Cloud Identity
and Access Management.

00:33:30.850 --> 00:33:34.450
It's a team of people working,
or an entire group of people,

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:37.180
so you set people up with
the right permissions.

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:41.110
And there's even a mobile app
for monitoring and managing

00:33:41.110 --> 00:33:43.180
your apps and
resources on the go.

00:33:46.130 --> 00:33:49.860
So while the console is
super powerful, flexible,

00:33:49.860 --> 00:33:53.610
you can also do everything
with a gcloud command line.

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:55.690
So for every action
in the console,

00:33:55.690 --> 00:33:57.860
there is a gcloud equivalent.

00:33:57.860 --> 00:34:03.470
So gcloud is our scriptable
and almighty CLI.

00:34:03.470 --> 00:34:08.190
Cloud Shell is a shell
environment hosted on GCP,

00:34:08.190 --> 00:34:11.860
and it manages your
projects and resources.

00:34:11.860 --> 00:34:13.679
It's accessible
from a web browser.

00:34:13.679 --> 00:34:16.320
And it's powered by a small
virtual machine with persistent

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:20.040
disk space, and
up-to-date software--

00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:24.030
Git, Docker,
containers-- I mean,

00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:27.960
compilers, all of these things
for all your development needs.

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:31.300
And it even comes with
a web code editor.

00:34:34.820 --> 00:34:38.190
So GCP resources are the
fundamental components that

00:34:38.190 --> 00:34:40.860
make up Google Cloud Services.

00:34:40.860 --> 00:34:44.550
Typical examples include Compute
Engine, virtual machines,

00:34:44.550 --> 00:34:48.120
Cloud Pub/Sub topics,
Cloud Storage buckets,

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:50.830
Cloud Functions, and so forth.

00:34:50.830 --> 00:34:53.190
And those resources
can be organized

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:55.960
into projects and folders.

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:59.460
This means, for instance, that
once you delete a project,

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:01.860
all the resources
attached to it can also

00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:05.610
be deleted, which is a great
way to keep a clean environment

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:07.200
and to keep your
costs under control.

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:11.590
So what do you need
to get started?

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:14.470
Well, the first thing you
need is a Google account.

00:35:14.470 --> 00:35:16.350
You can create a
new Google account,

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:20.780
or you can use an existing one,
such as your Gmail account.

00:35:20.780 --> 00:35:23.730
I would recommend that you
enable billing for your project

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:27.410
and that you sign up
for the $300 free trial

00:35:27.410 --> 00:35:30.410
to get started at no charge.

00:35:30.410 --> 00:35:32.690
If you do not sign up
for the free trial,

00:35:32.690 --> 00:35:36.560
you can still benefit from the
fairly generous, always free

00:35:36.560 --> 00:35:39.450
tier that GCP offers.

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:42.190
So $300 is actually
quite a bit of money,

00:35:42.190 --> 00:35:45.850
and enough to kick the tires
of GCP in a number of ways.

00:35:45.850 --> 00:35:50.170
You could have six VMs
running for one year--

00:35:50.170 --> 00:35:52.480
they're fairly small,
but that's six of them--

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:54.730
or a four Node
cluster of bigger VMs

00:35:54.730 --> 00:35:58.940
for a Container Engine, or
GKE cluster, for three months

00:35:58.940 --> 00:36:00.460
non-stop.

00:36:00.460 --> 00:36:04.510
Or you could store ten terabytes
in a multi-regional storage

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:07.600
bucket, which is the best
performing storage class,

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:08.240
for one month.

00:36:11.510 --> 00:36:13.940
The billing section
of the Cloud console

00:36:13.940 --> 00:36:15.710
is where you manage
billing accounts.

00:36:15.710 --> 00:36:18.500
And you link project to
those billing accounts.

00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:22.160
And a billing account is
really a payment method, one

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:24.673
or more credit cards or
bank account details.

00:36:24.673 --> 00:36:26.840
You can change a billing
account for a given project

00:36:26.840 --> 00:36:28.298
at any point of
time, and you could

00:36:28.298 --> 00:36:32.570
set budget alerts that helps
you manage cost, and set up

00:36:32.570 --> 00:36:36.980
triggered actions for
projects or accounts, as well.

00:36:36.980 --> 00:36:40.600
You can also generate billing
exports as well as reports

00:36:40.600 --> 00:36:42.550
to better understand your span.

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:47.840
This is important when you
start using Cloud at scale.

00:36:47.840 --> 00:36:51.050
So beyond the web console,
the command line tools,

00:36:51.050 --> 00:36:55.250
GCP also comes with a number
of built-in additional tools.

00:36:55.250 --> 00:36:57.500
To start with,
every project comes

00:36:57.500 --> 00:37:01.430
with a private by default
Get repo called Cloud Source

00:37:01.430 --> 00:37:05.810
repositories, which is free
for up to five users, and 50

00:37:05.810 --> 00:37:08.060
gigabytes of storage.

00:37:08.060 --> 00:37:11.610
So staying with resources that
are private to your projects

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:15.300
and teams, GCP also comes
with container repository

00:37:15.300 --> 00:37:17.760
to store your container images.

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:22.400
Once your container
image is in the repo,

00:37:22.400 --> 00:37:25.380
that means it's on
Google's network, which

00:37:25.380 --> 00:37:29.220
means, in turn, that the
deployment to GKE and Cloud Run

00:37:29.220 --> 00:37:32.500
are really fast.

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:35.340
And when it comes to building
container images, or any code,

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:37.680
for that matter,
there's Cloud Build,

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:40.350
a fully managed CI/CD platform.

00:37:40.350 --> 00:37:42.930
This includes building--
as the name implies--

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:46.455
but also deploying
to VMs, to GKE,

00:37:46.455 --> 00:37:47.580
and to serverless products.

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:55.290
Now, product naming, for us
and for everybody, is hard.

00:37:55.290 --> 00:37:57.270
But remembering
all of these names

00:37:57.270 --> 00:37:59.560
can be overwhelming
for you, as well.

00:37:59.560 --> 00:38:02.580
So here's a cheat sheet
with concise definitions

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:03.940
of all products.

00:38:03.940 --> 00:38:05.910
GCP in four words or less.

00:38:08.790 --> 00:38:12.350
And this covers every
product I've talked about

00:38:12.350 --> 00:38:14.540
but everyone, also,
I didn't talk about.

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:18.990
Now, as I come to the
end of the session,

00:38:18.990 --> 00:38:21.690
I'd like to do a shameless
plug for a series of videos

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:24.240
on the Google Cloud
YouTube channel called--

00:38:24.240 --> 00:38:26.920
no surprise-- GCP Essentials.

00:38:26.920 --> 00:38:29.820
The goal here is to cover--
in fairly short episodes--

00:38:29.820 --> 00:38:32.700
something that is
helpful for people

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:36.210
who are actually getting started
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:38:36.210 --> 00:38:37.530
Check out the video, please.

00:38:37.530 --> 00:38:41.660
And if you like
them, do subscribe.

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:44.270
So that was a lot
of ground covered.

00:38:44.270 --> 00:38:47.510
I'm leaving you with some links,
including the one for the four

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:49.160
words or less.

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:51.720
I hope this was time
well spent for you.

00:38:51.720 --> 00:38:54.620
I hope you will consider
bringing the awesomeness of GCP

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:57.110
to your existing
and upcoming apps.

00:38:57.110 --> 00:38:58.910
Thank you to everybody
on the live stream.

00:38:58.910 --> 00:39:01.377
And for everybody, feel free
to hit me up on Twitter.

00:39:01.377 --> 00:39:02.210
Thank you very much.

00:39:02.210 --> 00:39:03.110
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:03.110 --> 00:39:06.460
